sec.p:
The mean dose‐normalized plasma [14C]xevinapant and xevinapant concentration time courses following an IV microtracer (0.1 mg, 37 kBq) and oral (200 mg) administration of xevinapant are presented in Figure 3. Corresponding plasma PK parameters are summarized in Table 4. Following IV administration, maximum plasma concentrations of [14C]xevinapant and total radioactivity were observed at the first timepoint measured (5 min post‐IV bolus) for all six subjects, consistent with an IV bolus profile. Mean t1/2 of [14C]xevinapant was 6.69 h, while the mean t1/2 of total radioactivity was slightly longer: 10.1 h. Plasma concentrations of [14C]D‐1143‐MET1 were maximal at 4 h postdose (range: 2 to 5 h), and after tmax, declined through the last quantifiable timepoint at 47 h. The mean t1/2 was 9.16 h. The plasma CL of [14C]xevinapant was determined to be 12.2 L/h, which translates to a blood clearance of 21.0 L/h (based on a blood‐to‐plasma ratio of 0.58) or 0.269 L/h/kg (taking a mean body weight of 78.27 kg as determined in this study into account). The latter accounts for ≈21% of the human liver blood flow (1.26 L/h/kg
13
), indicating that xevinapant is a low clearance drug. The Vss for xevinapant was 75.3 L, being greater than total body water (42 L), suggesting that xevinapant was distributed in tissues.

The renal clearance is a minor contributor to the overall clearance of xevinapant, with a calculated percent contribution of < 20% relative to the systemic clearance (see below). The mean (CV%) renal clearance values of xevinapant and D‐1143‐MET1 following oral administration of xevinapant were 2.25 (31.1%) and 1.55 (33.1%) L/h, respectively. The corresponding calculated unbound renal clearance values were 255 and 251 mL/min, respectively, which are both approximately two times the typical glomerular filtration rate in healthy adults (i.e., ≈128 mL/min with a body weight of 70 kg
10
). These data indicate the contribution of active renal secretion to the renal clearance of both xevinapant and D‐1143‐MET1.
